---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/computer-science/intermediate/programming-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Programming is giving unambiguous instructions to a machine that does exactly what
    you say — not what you mean. Four constructs carry almost every program ever
    written: <strong>variables</strong> (remember), <strong>conditionals</strong> (decide),
    <strong>loops</strong> (repeat), and <strong>functions</strong> (package). Learn them
    in any language — Python is the friendliest first — and every other language is a
    dialect with different punctuation. This lesson adds the three structures the
    syllabus names alongside the concepts: <strong>arrays</strong> (the container that
    makes loops worth running), <strong>file handling</strong> (memory that survives the
    program ending), and the craft habits — error reading, tracing, naming — that
    separate working programs from demo programs.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unambiguity is the hard part, and it is worth naming precisely: a computer has
    no common sense to repair your intention. "Add up the prices" is fine for a human;
    a machine needs the initial value of the total, the order of iteration, and what to
    do at the last item. Everything in this lesson exists to translate intention into
    that level of precision without losing your mind — the constructs are the vocabulary,
    the arrays and files are the nouns, and the habits are the proofreading.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four constructs — a complete program's skeleton" widget="equationStep" config={progCfg} />

  <h2>Programming concepts: values, names and types</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>variable</strong> is a name bound to a value: <code>age = 17</code> stores
    17 under the name age, and every later mention of age fetches the current binding.
    A <strong>constant</strong> is the same idea with a promise not to change it —
    <code>MAX_SPEED = 120</code> written once in capitals — which documents intent and
    lets a change happen in exactly one place. <strong>Data types</strong> describe what
    operations make sense: integers (whole numbers), reals/floats (decimals), strings
    (text), characters, booleans (True/False). Types matter even in languages that
    don't enforce them: "5" + 3 is "53" in JavaScript (string concatenation) and a
    TypeError in Python — the same symbols, different type logic, a whole genre of bug
    prevented by knowing your data's type. <strong>Casting</strong> converts explicitly:
    int("5") + 3 is 8; str(5) + "3" is "53". Every input from a keyboard or file arrives
    as a string, so "read a number" always means "read text, then cast it" — a
    beginner's first runtime error, and a permanent professional reflex.
  </p>

  <h2>Selection and iteration: the two control flows</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Selection</strong> is the fork: <code>if score greater or equal 50: print
    "pass" else: print "fail"</code>, extended with elif chains for multiple cases and
    nested ifs for compound decisions. The discipline: conditions are boolean
    expressions (comparisons joined by and/or/not), and the classic bugs are boundary
    bugs — greater than versus greater-or-equal at exactly the pass mark. Write the
    boundary case as an explicit test: a score of exactly 50 must appear in your test
    data. <strong>Iteration</strong> comes in two shapes. Count-controlled loops
    (<code>for i in range(10)</code>) run a known number of times — the natural pair
    with arrays. Condition-controlled loops (<code>while</code>) run until a condition
    flips — the natural pair with input ("keep asking until valid") and with files
    ("until end of file"). The two classic failure modes are the infinite loop
    (the condition never becomes false — the update line is missing) and the loop that
    runs once too often. Both surrender to a trace table: write the loop variable's
    value in each row and watch the final iteration with your own eyes.
  </p>

  <h2>Arrays: the container that powers loops</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>array</strong> (Python calls its version a list) is an indexed sequence
    of values under one name: <code>scores = [72, 85, 91, 66]</code>, with items fetched
    by index — <code>scores[0]</code> is 72. Zero-based indexing is the convention to
    internalise: n elements occupy indexes 0 through n−1, so the loop
    <code>for i in range(len(scores))</code> touches each exactly once, and writing
    <code>scores[len(scores)]</code> is the canonical off-by-one crash. Arrays earn
    their place by making data and loops compose: the total pattern becomes three
    lines — total ← 0; for each score in the array: total ← total + score; average ←
    total / length. Two-dimensional arrays extend the idea to grids — a rainfall
    table of 52 weeks by 7 days is <code>rain[week][day]</code>, and summing a row
    (one week) versus the whole grid (the year) is a loop nested inside a loop,
    the outer walking weeks while the inner walks days. The syllabus's array
    operations are exactly these: traverse, search (linear), insert, delete — each
    one a two-line pattern once indexing is solid, and each one the same in pseudocode,
    Java, Python or VB.
  </p>

  <h2>Functions: packaging behaviour</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>function</strong> wraps steps under a name: define
    <code>average(scores)</code> once — total over length, rounded or not as you
    choose — and call it from anywhere. The mechanics matter: <strong>parameters</strong>
    are the function's named inputs; <strong>arguments</strong> are the actual values
    supplied at each call; the <strong>return value</strong> is the output handed back
    to the caller (<code>avg = average(readings)</code>). <strong>Scope</strong> is the
    rule that keeps this sane: variables created inside a function are local — they
    exist during the call and vanish after — so functions communicate through
    parameters and returns, not by reaching into each other's variables. That
    discipline is what makes functions reusable and testable in isolation: average()
    neither knows nor cares whether its array came from a keyboard, a file or another
    function. The design habit that follows: when a program grows past a page, it
    should be a set of small named functions, each doing one thing, with names that
    say what — <code>customer_total</code> over <code>x</code>; the computer does not
    care, but the human reading it in six months is you.
  </p>

  <h2>File handling: memory that outlives the program</h2>
  <p>
    Variables die with the process; files persist. The pattern is universal across
    languages: <strong>open</strong> a file in a mode (read, write, append), operate,
    <strong>close</strong> (or let a with-block close it for you). Write mode creates
    or truncates; append mode preserves existing contents and adds at the end — the
    difference between a report and a log, and a classic exam question. Reading runs
    to end-of-file: the while-not-EOF loop is the file twin of the while loop above.
    Text files are lines; CSV files are lines with comma-separated fields (open in any
    spreadsheet — the data-science lessons' raw material); records with mixed fields
    get split on read and joined on write. Worked shape: read every line of
    results.csv, split each line on commas, cast the third field to a number, total
    and count — and you have the standard "process a data file" algorithm that the
    data-science strand will spend whole lessons industrialising.
  </p>

  <h2>The craft: errors, tracing, small-and-working</h2>
  <p>
    Errors come in three families with three responses. <strong>Syntax errors</strong>
    (missing colon, unbalanced bracket) are caught before anything runs — read the
    message, fix the line. <strong>Runtime errors</strong> (dividing by zero, index
    out of range, casting "hello" to int) crash mid-run — the traceback names the file,
    line and complaint, and beginners who fear red text learn to read it first.
    <strong>Logic errors</strong> are the worst: the program runs and gives wrong
    answers — no message will ever help, and the defence is the algorithm lesson's
    trace table plus the boundary-test discipline. The habits that bind the lesson
    together: trace by hand before opening a debugger (most bugs surrender to manual
    tracing); keep types in mind even in loose languages; name things for readers,
    not for the machine; and grow programs small-and-working — write two lines, run
    them, repeat. Programs are grown, not written; every large working program is a
    small working program that was never allowed to stop working.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked program: from spec to working code</h2>
  <p>
    Assemble the constructs on one spec, because the assembly is the skill. Spec:
    "Read n temperatures, then report the highest, the lowest and the average."
    Decompose: read the count; read the values into an array; compute max (seed with
    the first, replace on bigger); compute min (same logic, mirrored); compute average
    (total divided by count, guarded for count zero — the boundary case again); print
    all three. Now map each piece to a construct: the reading is a count-controlled
    loop filling an array; the statistics are a single traversal loop doing three
    updates; the printing is selection for formatting. Write it in pseudocode first —
    every line unambiguous — then type it, run it on normal data ([10, 20, 30] → max
    30, min 10, avg 20), then boundary data (one value; all equal values; n = 0), then
    erroneous data (a letter at the prompt). Total: about fifteen lines. The
    instructive part is not the program — it is that every design decision was one of
    this lesson's patterns, and every test was one of the algorithm lesson's three
    data classes. The craft is the composition, not the vocabulary.
  </p>

  <h2>Beyond arrays: records and key-value structures</h2>
  <p>
    Arrays hold values of one kind under numeric indexes; two structures extend them
    toward real data. A <strong>record</strong> bundles named fields of mixed types —
    a student is name (string), age (integer), grades (array) — so one unit travels
    through the program: read a record, store records in an array, sort the array by
    any field. This is exactly the shape of a CSV row (split on read, joined on
    write), which is why files-of-records is the bridge between this lesson and the
    data-science strand: a spreadsheet is an array of records that someone else
    already typed. A <strong>key-value structure</strong> (dictionary, map, hash)
    indexes by meaning instead of position — ages["Amara"] instead of ages[3] —
    trading order for instant lookup by name. Arrays answer "what is at position
    i?"; dictionaries answer "what do we know about X?"; records group the X's
    attributes; and files persist all of it. Almost every program ever written is
    some arrangement of those four moves.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements for the curious. <strong>The named-box model is a
    simplification</strong>: in Python, names are labels bound to objects, not boxes
    holding values — so assignment copies the binding, not the object, and two names
    can label one list (mutate it through either and both see the change). This is
    call-by-object-reference, and it explains bugs that surprise everyone exactly
    once. <strong>Zero-indexing has a logic</strong>: an index is an offset from the
    start — element i lives i slots past the beginning — which makes ranges
    half-open and composition cleaner; it is a design choice, not an accident
    (Fortran and Pascal index from 1; the machine-index-from-zero lineage won).
    <strong>Interpreted versus compiled</strong>: Python translates and executes line
    by line (fast to develop, slower to run); C-family languages compile to machine
    code first (slower to develop, faster to run) — and the logic-gates lesson shows
    what the compiled code finally becomes: voltages through gates. The stack closes:
    four constructs, two data structures, one file discipline — everything above is
    arrangement.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Python Software Foundation tutorial; Kernighan &amp; Ritchie, The C Programming Language (the classic); Cambridge IGCSE/A Level Computer Science programming syllabus sections.</p>
